    problems: [
      {
        title: 'Got all my servo\'s in last night (using decent quality metal gear…',
        problem: 'Got all my servo\'s in last night (using decent quality metal gear servo\'s for the elevons..don\'t need a servo failure over water!) and the elevons and pushrods are in, setup and trimmed.',
        fix: 'The thread does not record a fix for this.',
        author: 'JettaManDan', date: 'Feb 03, 2010',
      },
      {
        title: 'I know well of what you speak when your referring to the tabs and…',
        problem: 'I know well of what you speak when your referring to the tabs and slots problem.I end up having to resize all the tabs and slots on the plans that I use anyways because of the thickness of the foam sheets that I have available to me.I use depapered commercial grade art board with the paper layers removed and it has worked very well for these smaller builds.It is way more work for me than using standard Depron sheets but it is a lot more cost effective in the end as Depron is expensive and single sourced around here(the LHS $$$$$).Carbon fibre rods are only available from the LHS(Midwest Products line)and are quite pricey as well.Oh,to beloaded...sigh....',
        fix: 'The thread does not record a fix for this.',
        author: 'Turbobeaver', date: 'Apr 16, 2010',
      },
      {
        title: 'We both put the ESC\'s on the outside on both the Seadart and the…',
        problem: 'We both put the ESC\'s on the outside on both the Seadart and the Space shuttle - problem solved.',
        fix: 'I dropped a smear of bath sealent in the ESC ends and have since had the ESC in the water after the Seadart had flipped with no consequence to ESC (All was well once the motor had dryed out I think cooling is important when you are running 29amps constant though a 30amp ESC - my RCTimer combo produces 325watts on APCE 6x4 with the RCtimer 2212/06 and 30Amp ESC - admittedly I have had to re-epoxy the magnets in once the original glue had melted and magnets had started to move .',
        author: 'siddus', date: 'Jun 17, 2010',
      },
      {
        title: 'I pretty much resigned myself to the fact the problem was user error…',
        problem: 'I pretty much resigned myself to the fact the problem was user error but that was no help cleaning up the mess.',
        fix: 'The thread does not record a fix for this.',
        author: 'glewis', date: 'Jun 23, 2010',
      },
      {
        title: 'fiberglass cloth on the bottom hull/ski to toughen it up from…',
        problem: 'fiberglass cloth on the bottom hull/ski to toughen it up from potential ground rock damage as well.I used a craft acrylics navy blue and it didn\'t cover well in one coat so then it needed a second coat.Then I discovered that the acrylic paint is not totally water proof and had to be clear coated with water based poly to prevent the blue from coming off.AHHH!!!This model also has a full rudder as well as the water rudder so each and every little thing you add or do is more weight and it just creeps up on you.It is amazing just how much weight paint can add to a model.I\'m still playing with the balance issue to see if I can improve upon it but my gut feeling is that this thing just needs more power.One thing I can say about this plane is that it is durable as hell in its present state.Some of the test flight attempts ended pretty violently and this thing came away with a small crack in the fin top and that was it.The nose of this plane should look like a Lawrence Welk accordion by now but it just keeps on truckin.I keep picking it up after every flight attempt expecting to see the nose missing or shortened....but she\'s still there.HA!HA!I\'ll see if I can get some pics up of the finished model for everyone to see.Maybe I\'m missing something.Even if it doesn\'t fly,I will have learned something from this experience and know better next time around.As long as there is a supply of foam sheet available,there is always hope............next time.',
        fix: 'The thread does not record a fix for this.',
        author: 'Turbobeaver', date: 'Nov 11, 2010',
      },
    ],
